Package: taxodist
Title: Taxonomic Hierarchy Distances and Lineage Analysis
Version: 1.1.0
LazyData: true
Authors@R: person("Rodrigo Fonseca", "Villa", email = "rodrigo03.villa@gmail.com",
           role = c("aut", "cre"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0009-0005-2938-2270"))
Maintainer: Rodrigo Fonseca Villa <rodrigo03.villa@gmail.com>
Description: Computes distances between taxonomic hierarchy nodes using lineage
    data retrieved from The Taxonomicon <http://taxonomicon.taxonomy.nl>, the
    Open Tree of Life reference taxonomy (Rees and Cranston, 2017)
    <doi:10.3897/BDJ.5.e12581>, or the NCBI Taxonomy (Schoch et al., 2020)
    <doi:10.1093/database/baaa062>. For distinct nodes, distance is defined as
    the reciprocal of the depth of their most recent common ancestor; identical
    nodes have distance zero. This definition yields an ultrametric within each
    connected hierarchy. Path length, Wu-Palmer, and rank-based distances are
    also available, together with the taxonomic diversity and distinctness
    indices of Clarke and Warwick (1998) <doi:10.1046/j.1365-2664.1998.3540523.x>
    and their randomization test, the taxonomic dissimilarities between samples
    of Clarke, Somerfield and Chapman (2006) <doi:10.1016/j.jembe.2005.12.017>,
    and standardized effect sizes of mean pairwise and nearest-taxon distances
    (Webb et al., 2002) <doi:10.1146/annurev.ecolsys.33.010802.150448>.
    Functions are provided for auditable name resolution, comparison of
    classifications, online or user-supplied lineage analysis, node ranks,
    clade membership, pairwise, matrix, and cross-set distance calculation,
    classification trees with named clades, hierarchical clustering, principal
    coordinates analysis, portable and comparable JSON analysis bundles, and
    cache management. Distance matrices are returned as base R 'dist' objects.
    The distances represent classification depth rather than evolutionary time
    or phylogenetic branch length.
